<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Dee, Wales</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Dee rises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snowdonia">Snowdonia</a> and flows through Bala Lake, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llangollen">Llangollen</a> and the English city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> before entering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> through a broad estuary between the Wirral and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flintshire">Flintshire</a>. For much of its course it forms or follows the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built their fortress of Deva at Chester on its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-dee-wales.jpg" alt="River Dee, Wales" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Akke at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Llangollenviewfromstation.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The River Dee, in Welsh Afon Dyfrdwy, has its source on the slopes of Dduallt above Llanuwchllyn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snowdonia">Snowdonia</a>. It runs northeast through the Vale of Edeyrnion to Corwen, then through the forested valley past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llangollen">Llangollen</a>, where it flows under a 16th-century bridge counted among the Seven Wonders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and, further east, below Thomas Telford's Pontcysyllte Aqueduct of 1805. Beyond that it forms the border between Wales and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and meanders across the Cheshire Plain to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a>. The section from Bala to Chester is 113 kilometres long, and the catchment down to Chester Weir covers 1,816.8 square kilometres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Dee was the traditional boundary of the Kingdom of Gwynedd for centuries, possibly since its founding in the 5th century. A 13th-century form of the name, flumen Dubr Duiu, appears to derive from the Brythonic dēvā, River of the Goddess or Holy River, and the river is personified as the goddess Aerfen. Its name inspired that of the Roman fortress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Deva Victrix</a>.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> the Old Dee Bridge dates from about 1387, on the site of wooden predecessors reaching back to the Roman period. Chester Weir was built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-davranches-earl-of-chester">Hugh Lupus</a> to power corn mills, and the Roodee racecourse stands on the site of the Roman harbour, which silted up. From the end of the 17th century sediment kept larger ships from the city and its trade declined. Between 1732 and 1736 the Dee Navigation Company, using engineers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and local money, dug a straight channel west of Chester and diverted the river into it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> the canalised river passes Deeside, a region of heavy industry, before widening into an estuary 23 kilometres long that enters the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a>. Its mudflats and saltmarsh make it important for birdlife, and it is a Site of Special Scientific Interest and a Ramsar site. The river is also a Special Area of Conservation for Atlantic salmon and floating water plantain. A system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>, the Dee Regulation Scheme, stores winter water and releases it in summer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “River Dee, Wales” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Deceangli lived in what is now Flintshire and Denbighshire, between the <b>Dee</b> estuary and the Clwyd valley, a region of hillforts such as Moel Hiraddug and Penycloddiau.</blockquote><a href="/state/deceangli">Deceangli</a></li></ul></section>
